<!DOCTYPE service-group SYSTEM "avahi-service.dtd">

<!--Announce this display on the network.

     Until now the only way to find a display was to sweep the subnet for an
     open port and ask every answer whether it was a kiosk. That is slow, it
     depends on guessing the right port, and it finds nothing on a network
     that is not the one being swept.

     avahi is already installed and running on this image, so this costs one
     file and no processes. Anything that browses _steadyscreen._tcp gets
     every display, by name and address, straight away:

         avahi-browse -rt _steadyscreen._tcp

     _http._tcp is advertised as well so the display also turns up in the
     ordinary "network devices" lists people already have.

     PORT 80 IS ASSERTED HERE. The client serves 80 and only falls back to
     8080 when an image is too old to bind it — a machine in that state
     advertises an address that will not answer, which is the correct
     complaint to raise about a machine that needs attention anyway. -->

<service-group>
  <name replace-wildcards="yes">SteadyScreen on %h</name>

  <service>
    <type>_steadyscreen._tcp</type>
    <port>80</port>
    <txt-record>path=/</txt-record>
    <txt-record>admin=/</txt-record>
  </service>

  <service>
    <type>_http._tcp</type>
    <port>80</port>
    <txt-record>path=/</txt-record>
  </service>
</service-group>
